  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Enamel hypoplasia is the inadequate deposition of normal enamel matrix, affecting one or several teeth.",
              "pdf_page": 523,
              "printed_page": 441,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The crowns can have areas of normal enamel next to hypoplastic or missing enamel.",
              "pdf_page": 523,
              "printed_page": 441,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Apparent defect in enamel surfaces, often pitted and discolored; focal or generalized, due to disruption of normal enamel formation.",
              "pdf_page": 523,
              "printed_page": 441,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most cases are primarily aesthetic; some have extensive structural damage, even root involvement. The dentin is rarely exposed with hypoplasia.",
              "pdf_page": 523,
              "printed_page": 441,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Enamel hypoplasia is less common than hypocalcification.",
              "pdf_page": 523,
              "printed_page": 441,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Enamel hypocalcification refers to inadequate mineralization (calcification) of enamel, affecting several or all teeth. The crowns are covered by poorly formed, rough enamel that may easily be worn or flaked away and have light brown discoloration. There can be areas of normal enamel next to areas of abnormal enamel.",
              "pdf_page": 523,
              "printed_page": 441,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Systemic influences during enamel formation (e.g., distemper, fever) over an extended time may cause generalized changes; local or focal influences (e.g., trauma, even from deciduous tooth extraction) over a short time may cause specific patterns or bands.",
              "pdf_page": 523,
              "printed_page": 441,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Teeth may be more sensitive with exposed dentin, and occasionally fractures of severely compromised teeth occur; usually they remain fully functional.",
              "pdf_page": 523,
              "printed_page": 441,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
